\title{Guide to Assembly Attributes in .NET}
\author{}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This document provides a comprehensive guide on using assembly attributes in .NET to manage assembly metadata. It covers the purpose and usage of the most common attributes, offering clear explanations and examples for software developers familiar with .NET.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Assembly attributes are a mechanism in .NET to embed metadata directly into an assembly. These attributes are typically placed in a special file named \texttt{AssemblyInfo.cs} (or defined in the project file for newer SDK-style projects) and are used to provide information such as version, copyright, company, and more. This metadata is utilized by the .NET runtime, development tools, and operating system features (e.g., Windows File Properties). Properly setting assembly attributes ensures consistent versioning, accurate attribution, and correct COM interop behavior.

\section{Common Assembly Attributes}
The following subsections describe the most frequently used assembly attributes, their purpose, and how to apply them.

\subsection{AssemblyTitle}
\textbf{Purpose:} Specifies a friendly name for the assembly. This name can differ from the physical file name and is often displayed in Windows File Properties and other user interfaces.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyTitle("MyApplication")]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{AssemblyDescription}
\textbf{Purpose:} Provides a brief description of the assembly’s purpose or functionality. This description appears in Windows File Properties and can be useful for documentation.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyDescription("Core library for data access.")]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{AssemblyCompany}
\textbf{Purpose:} Identifies the company or organization that developed the assembly.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyCompany("Contoso Ltd.")]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{AssemblyProduct}
\textbf{Purpose:} Specifies the product name to which the assembly belongs. This is typically the overall product name, as opposed to the individual component name.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyProduct("Contoso Suite")]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{AssemblyCopyright}
\textbf{Purpose:} Contains copyright information for the assembly.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyCopyright("Copyright © Contoso 2025")]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{AssemblyTrademark}
\textbf{Purpose:} Contains trademark information related to the assembly or product.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyTrademark("Contoso™")]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{ComVisible}
\textbf{Purpose:} Controls whether the assembly is visible to COM (Component Object Model) clients. Setting this to \texttt{false} hides the assembly from COM, which is the default for most pure .NET assemblies. If COM interop is required, specific types can be individually exposed.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: ComVisible(false)]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{AssemblyVersion}
\textbf{Purpose:} Defines the version number that the .NET runtime uses for assembly binding and versioning. This version is critical for strong naming and side-by-side execution. It usually follows the \texttt{major.minor.build.revision} format, but any string is allowed (though a four-part numeric version is expected by the runtime).

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyVersion("1.0.0.0")]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{AssemblyFileVersion}
\textbf{Purpose:} Specifies the version number that appears in Windows File Properties. Unlike \texttt{AssemblyVersion}, this attribute does not affect runtime binding. It is often set to the same value as \texttt{AssemblyVersion} but can differ to reflect a separate file versioning scheme.

\textbf{Usage:}
\begin{lstlisting}
[assembly: AssemblyFileVersion("1.0.0.0")]
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Additional Notes}
In addition to the attributes above, you may encounter \texttt{AssemblyInformationalVersion}, which represents the product version and can include additional information such as prerelease labels (e.g., \texttt{"1.0.0-beta"}). This attribute is often used for display purposes.

For .NET Core, .NET 5+, and modern SDK-style projects, many of these attributes can be set directly in the \texttt{.csproj} file using properties like \texttt{<AssemblyVersion>}, \texttt{<FileVersion>}, and \texttt{<InformationalVersion>}. However, the underlying attribute mechanism remains the same.

\section{Conclusion}
Properly managing assembly metadata is essential for maintaining consistency, enabling correct versioning, providing legal attribution, and controlling COM visibility. By using the described assembly attributes, developers can ensure their .NET assemblies are correctly identified and versioned throughout the development lifecycle. This guide has covered the core attributes and their usage; applying them correctly will contribute to professional, maintainable .NET applications.

\end{document}
