# Creating a Simple Student Name Addition Form with PHP, Smarty, and Bootstrap

Web forms are the backbone of interactive web applications, allowing users to submit data that can be stored, processed, and later retrieved. For developers, building a form that seamlessly integrates with a database while maintaining a clean separation of logic and presentation is a fundamental skill. This article provides a step-by-step guide to creating a simple HTML form that adds student names to a MySQL database using PHP for the backend and the Smarty template engine for presentation. We’ll also use Bootstrap to style the form quickly and responsively. By the end, you’ll have a functional, user-friendly form that demonstrates best practices in form handling and data management.

---

## Step 1: Database Setup

Before writing any code, we need a database and a table to store the student names. We’ll assume you have a MySQL server running (e.g., via XAMPP, WAMP, or a remote server). Create a database named `school` and a table `students` with an auto-incrementing ID and a name field.

```sql
CREATE DATABASE school;
USE school;

CREATE TABLE students (
    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
```

The `created_at` timestamp is optional but useful for tracking when records are added.

---

## Step 2: Install and Configure Smarty

Smarty is a template engine that separates PHP logic from HTML markup. Download the latest version from [smarty.net](https://www.smarty.net/) and extract it into your project folder. A typical structure looks like this:

```
project/
│
├── libs/               # Smarty library files
├── templates/          # .tpl files (templates)
├── templates_c/        # Compiled templates (Smarty will create)
├── cache/              # Cache files (optional)
└── config/             # Configuration files (optional)
```

Now create a configuration file, e.g., `config.php`, to initialize Smarty and set the necessary directories.

```php
<?php
// config.php

// Include the Smarty class
require_once('libs/Smarty.class.php');

// Create a new Smarty instance
$smarty = new Smarty();

// Set directory paths
$smarty->setTemplateDir('templates/');
$smarty->setCompileDir('templates_c/');
$smarty->setCacheDir('cache/');
$smarty->setConfigDir('config/');

// Optionally enable debugging during development
// $smarty->debugging = true;
```

Ensure that the directories `templates`, `templates_c`, and `cache` exist and are writable by the web server.

---

## Step 3: Building the HTML Form with Bootstrap

Inside the `templates` directory, create a file named `add_student.tpl`. This file will contain the HTML form and will be rendered by Smarty. We’ll use Bootstrap 5 to style the form quickly. Include the Bootstrap CSS via its CDN in the `<head>` section.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Add Student</title>
    <!-- Bootstrap CSS CDN -->
    <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.0/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet">
</head>
<body class="container mt-5">

    <h1 class="mb-4">Add a New Student</h1>

    <form method="post" action="add.php" class="mb-3">
        <div class="mb-3">
            <label for="student_name" class="form-label">Student Name</label>
            <input type="text" class="form-control" id="student_name" name="student_name" placeholder="Enter full name" required>
        </div>
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Submit</button>
    </form>

    {if $message}
        <div class="alert {if $success}alert-success{else}alert-danger{/if}">
            {$message}
        </div>
    {/if}

</body>
</html>
```

Notice the Smarty tags `{if}` and `{$message}`. We’ll use these to display success or error messages after form processing. The `required` attribute on the input provides basic client-side validation, preventing the form from being submitted empty. However, server-side validation is still essential.

---

## Step 4: Handling Form Submission with PHP

Create a PHP file, e.g., `add.php`, that will process the form, interact with the database, and render the template.

```php
<?php
// add.php

// Include Smarty configuration
require_once('config.php');

// Database connection details
$host = 'localhost';
$dbname = 'school';
$username = 'root';     // change as needed
$password = '';         // change as needed

// Initialize variables
$message = '';
$success = false;

// Check if the form was submitted
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST' && isset($_POST['student_name'])) {
    // Sanitize and validate input
    $name = trim($_POST['student_name']);
    
    if (empty($name)) {
        $message = 'Student name cannot be empty.';
    } else {
        try {
            // Connect to the database using PDO
            $pdo = new PDO("mysql:host=$host;dbname=$dbname;charset=utf8", $username, $password);
            $pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);

            // Prepare and execute the INSERT statement
            $stmt = $pdo->prepare("INSERT INTO students (name) VALUES (:name)");
            $stmt->bindParam(':name', $name);
            $stmt->execute();

            $message = 'Student added successfully!';
            $success = true;

            // Optionally clear the input after success
            $name = '';

        } catch (PDOException $e) {
            $message = 'Database error: ' . $e->getMessage();
        }
    }
}

// Assign variables to Smarty
$smarty->assign('message', $message);
$smarty->assign('success', $success);

// Display the template
$smarty->display('add_student.tpl');
```

In this script, we first include the Smarty configuration. Then we check for a POST request and the presence of the `student_name` field. After sanitizing (trimming) the input, we perform server-side validation to ensure it’s not empty. If validation passes, we attempt to insert the record into the database using PDO, which helps prevent SQL injection. Depending on the outcome, we set an appropriate message and a success flag.

Finally, we assign the message and success flag to Smarty variables and display the template.

---

## Step 5: Displaying Messages and Rendering the Template

The template already contains the logic to show the message. The Smarty tags `{if $message}` check whether a message exists. The `{$message}` variable outputs the text, and the `{if $success}` condition applies the appropriate Bootstrap alert class (`alert-success` or `alert-danger`). This gives the user immediate feedback after form submission.

When the page is loaded without a POST request, the form appears empty and no message is shown.

---

## Step 6: Client-Side and Server-Side Validation

Validation is crucial to maintain data integrity. The `required` attribute in the HTML input field provides a quick check on the client side, improving user experience by preventing obviously incomplete submissions. However, client-side validation can be bypassed (e.g., by disabling JavaScript or manipulating the DOM), so server-side validation is mandatory.

In our PHP code, we validated that the name is not empty after trimming. You could extend this validation to check length, allowed characters, or even duplicate names, depending on your requirements. Always sanitize user input before using it in database queries; using prepared statements with PDO (or MySQLi) automatically handles escaping, which protects against SQL injection.

---

## Conclusion

Creating a simple form to add student names demonstrates the core concepts of handling user input, interacting with a database, and separating presentation from logic using Smarty. By incorporating Bootstrap, we ensured the form is visually appealing and responsive with minimal effort. Effective form design—including clear labels, instant feedback, and robust validation—greatly enhances the user experience and contributes to better data management.

This example can be extended in many ways: listing existing students, editing records, or adding more fields. The principles remain the same, providing a solid foundation for building more complex web applications.

